## Programming and Ada Lovelace

The theoretical capability of the Analytical Engine attracted the attention of Augusta Ada King, Countess of Lovelace (Ada Lovelace). In 1843, she translated a French article about the machine written by Italian engineer Luigi Menabrea. At Babbage's request, Lovelace appended her own extensive annotations to the translation, which ended up being three times the length of the original article.

In "Note G" of her appendices, Lovelace detailed a step-by-step sequence of operations for the Analytical Engine to calculate Bernoulli numbers. Because this was the first published algorithm specifically tailored for implementation on a computing machine, Lovelace is widely recognized as the world's first computer programmer. Furthermore, she recognized the theoretical implications of the machine far beyond Babbage's original mathematical focus. She theorized that because the engine operated on abstract symbols (the gears could represent something other than quantities), it could theoretically be used to compose complex music or process alphabetical logic, conceptually anticipating the transition from calculation to general-purpose computation.

## Partial Construction and Modern Reconstruction

Babbage spent the latter half of his life continuously refining the design of the Analytical Engine, producing thousands of pages of detailed mechanical drawings. However, the British government had already spent £17,000 on the failed Difference Engine project and refused to fund the immensely more complex Analytical Engine.

Following Babbage's death in 1871, his youngest son, Henry Provost Babbage, constructed a partial working model of the Mill and the printing mechanism using his father's designs. In 1910, this partial machine successfully calculated and printed a list of multiples of pi.

In the 21st century, an initiative known as Plan 28 was launched by computer historian Doron Swade and a team of researchers. The project aims to thoroughly analyze Babbage's extensive, often-modified archive of mechanical blueprints in order to construct the first fully working, complete physical model of the Analytical Engine, finally realizing Babbage's 1837 vision.

